/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Explorers & Inventions (batch z) */
WB.L("shackleton-endurance", "explore", "beginner", "Shackleton and the Endurance",
  "His ship was crushed by ice and his men were stranded at the bottom of the world. What happened next became the most famous survival story in polar history.",
  [
    ["A plan, and a myth", "Shackleton planned the first crossing of Antarctica and chose his crew for temperament as well as skill.",
      "By 1914 Amundsen had reached the South Pole and Scott had died returning from it. Ernest Shackleton wanted a new prize: the first crossing of Antarctica on foot, from the Weddell Sea to the Ross Sea, about 2,900 kilometres. His ship Endurance sailed from England in August 1914, just as the First World War began. The Admiralty told him to carry on.\n\nThe famous \"Men wanted for hazardous journey\" advertisement is almost certainly a myth: researchers have found no trace of it in the archives. In reality thousands reportedly applied, and Shackleton chose men through quick, quirky interviews, valuing cheerfulness under strain as much as skill."],
    ["Trapped in the ice", "Endurance was frozen into the pack ice and crushed, leaving the crew camped on drifting floes with one goal: getting home alive.",
      "In January 1915, still short of the Antarctic coast, Weddell Sea pack ice closed around Endurance. The 28 men played football on the floes and held concerts while the ship drifted through the polar winter. Then the pressure grew and the hull buckled. In October Shackleton ordered everyone onto the ice, and in November Endurance sank. The goal became getting every man home alive.\n\nThe crew camped on moving ice with three lifeboats, and the sled dogs had to be shot. Photographer Frank Hurley, an Australian, kept a few glass plate photographs and smashed the rest so he would not be tempted to go back. In April 1916 the ice broke up and they took to the boats."],
    ["Elephant Island and the Caird", "With no hope of discovery on Elephant Island, Shackleton and five men sailed a small lifeboat 1,300 kilometres to South Georgia.",
      "After about a week at sea the boats reached Elephant Island in mid April 1916, the first solid ground in about 16 months. But it lay far from any shipping route and nobody knew they were there. So Shackleton took five men in the James Caird, a lifeboat under 7 metres long that the ship's carpenter had strengthened and partly decked over. The other 22, led by Frank Wild, sheltered under two upturned boats and ate penguins and seals.\n\nOn 24 April the six set out for South Georgia, 1,300 kilometres away. Frank Worsley navigated with only a handful of sun sightings. The men were soaked, chipped ice off the boat and survived a hurricane force storm. After about 16 days they landed."],
    ["Over the mountains", "Shackleton crossed South Georgia's unmapped interior to raise the alarm, and all 22 men on Elephant Island were eventually rescued.",
      "They had landed on the wrong side of South Georgia, and the whaling stations lay on the far coast. The boat was too battered to sail around. So Shackleton, Worsley and Tom Crean crossed the island's unmapped glaciers and mountains, with screws in their boots for grip, a rope and almost no rest. After about 36 hours they reached the Stromness whaling station in May 1916.\n\nShackleton then made several attempts to reach Elephant Island, but ice turned him back. On 30 August 1916 the Chilean tug Yelcho, commanded by Luis Pardo, finally got through. All 22 men were alive. Sadly, the expedition's separate Ross Sea party, on the far side of Antarctica, lost three men."],
    ["The wreck is found", "Shackleton's expedition failed at its goals but is remembered for leadership, and in 2022 Endurance was found, well preserved, about 3,000 metres down.",
      "For over a century Endurance lay somewhere beneath the Weddell Sea, so choked with ice that searching seemed nearly impossible. In March 2022 an expedition using an icebreaker and underwater robots located the wreck about 3,000 metres down. The wooden hull was largely intact, and the name ENDURANCE could still be read across the stern. Cold water and the absence of the wood-eating creatures found in warmer seas had protected it.\n\nThe wreck is a protected historic site under the Antarctic Treaty and is being left undisturbed. Shackleton's expedition failed at everything it set out to do, yet it is remembered for something rarer: a leader who brought every man of Endurance home."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the truth about the \"Men wanted for hazardous journey\" advertisement?", ["It ran in newspapers for a year and drew thousands of replies","It was written by Shackleton at the request of the Admiralty","No trace of it exists in the archives, so it is probably a myth","It was real, but it was only ever posted in port towns"], 2, "The famous advert is a wonderful piece of writing, but no original has ever turned up."],
    [1, "Why did the expedition's goal change in 1915?", ["Endurance was crushed and sank, so survival became the goal","The war ended and the Admiralty recalled the ship","The crew ran out of fuel at South Georgia","Amundsen announced he would make the crossing first"], 0, "Once the ship was lost, survival replaced the crossing as the goal."],
    [2, "Why did Shackleton sail the James Caird to South Georgia?", ["The island had no food, so the men had to leave","The Admiralty had ordered them to meet a ship there","The men on the island had grown tired of waiting","Nobody knew they were there, far from any shipping route"], 3, "Waiting for rescue on Elephant Island meant almost certain death, so help had to be fetched."],
    [3, "Why did Shackleton cross South Georgia's mountains on foot?", ["The crew wanted to map the unexplored interior","They had landed on the wrong side and the boat was too battered","The James Caird had been left behind on Elephant Island","The whalers had told them a ship waited inland"], 1, "The only way to reach the whaling station was across unmapped glaciers and peaks."],
    [4, "Why was the Endurance wreck so well preserved?", ["It was buried deep under thick layers of mud and sand","It lay in shallow water covered by permanent sea ice","Cold water and no wood-eating creatures, unlike warmer seas","Divers had recovered and restored it after 1915"], 2, "The cold, ice-choked Weddell Sea kept the wooden hull largely intact for over a century."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The famous Shackleton recruiting advert probably never existed."});

WB.L("safety-elevator", "explore", "beginner", "The safety lift: how Otis made skyscrapers possible",
  "Lifting people up a shaft was easy. Convincing them they would not plunge to their deaths was the hard part, and one man did it by cutting his own rope.",
  [
    ["Why lifts were deadly", "People hoisted loads for thousands of years but avoided riding them, because a snapped rope meant a straight fall.",
      "Lifting with ropes and pulleys is ancient. Builders hauled stone, mines raised ore, and in Rome's Colosseum, wooden lifts worked by teams of men raised wild animals into the arena. By the early nineteenth century, steam-powered hoists moved goods between factory floors.\n\nPeople rarely rode them. Hemp ropes wore out and snapped, and when they did, the platform and everything on it fell straight down the shaft. A broken rope on a goods hoist meant smashed crates. On a passenger lift it would mean funerals. Building a lift that people would trust was the real challenge."],
    ["Otis's safety brake", "Elisha Otis's 1852 brake used a spring and ratchet teeth to catch the platform the moment its rope went slack.",
      "Elisha Otis was an American mechanic from Vermont who had tried businesses from wagon building to running a sawmill. In 1852 he was working at a bedstead factory in Yonkers, New York, and was asked to help install a hoist to lift heavy equipment. Worried about what would happen if the rope failed, he devised a safety brake.\n\nHe attached a strong wagon spring to the top of the platform, linked to the hoisting rope. While the rope was taut, the spring was held in. If the rope broke and the tension vanished, the spring snapped outwards and its ends caught in ratchet teeth cut into the guide rails on either side of the shaft."],
    ["All safe, gentlemen", "Otis won public trust by having his own rope cut in front of a crowd, and the first passenger safety lift opened in 1857.",
      "A clever brake is useless if nobody trusts it, and Otis understood showmanship. At an exhibition in New York's Crystal Palace in 1854, he rode an open platform high above the crowd, then had an assistant cut the only rope with an axe. The platform dropped a few centimetres and stopped dead. Otis is said to have announced, \"All safe, gentlemen, all safe.\" Whether or not those were his exact words, the stunt worked, and orders arrived.\n\nIn March 1857 the first commercial passenger safety lift opened in the E. V. Haughwout Building, a five storey store on Broadway. Steam-powered, it climbed about twelve metres a minute. Otis died in 1861, but his sons built the company into a giant, with lifts around the world, including the Eiffel Tower."],
    ["Upside-down buildings", "Safe lifts and steel frames made skyscrapers possible and turned top floors from the cheapest into the most prestigious.",
      "Before lifts, buildings were limited by how many stairs people would climb, usually five or six storeys. The top floors were the least desirable, often rented cheaply to servants, poor tenants or struggling artists. Safe lifts changed that. Combined with steel frames, which carry far more weight than thick masonry walls, they let cities build upwards, and the skyscraper was born. Chicago's ten storey Home Insurance Building, finished in 1885, is often called the first.\n\nElectric lifts soon replaced steam and hydraulic ones; Werner von Siemens demonstrated one in Germany in 1880. The view, the quiet and the distance from street noise made the top floors the most prestigious addresses in town."],
    ["How lifts stay safe", "Modern lifts layer several independent protections, so deadly falls are extremely rare, and if you are stuck you should stay inside and wait for help.",
      "Modern traction lifts hang from several steel cables, each strong enough alone to hold the car. A counterweight balances the car, so the motor lifts only the difference. If the car goes too fast, an overspeed governor triggers brakes that clamp onto the guide rails, a descendant of Otis's idea. Buffers cushion the car at the bottom. In 1945, after a bomber hit the Empire State Building, operator Betty Lou Oliver survived a fall of about 75 storeys, partly because trailing cables and compressed air slowed it.\n\nSupertall towers use sky lobbies, where passengers change lifts, and lighter carbon fibre cables. If you ever get stuck, press the alarm or intercom button, stay inside and wait for trained help. Never try to climb out. For a medical emergency, call 000."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did people avoid riding hoists before Otis?", ["Steam engines regularly exploded inside the shaft","Hoists were far too weak to carry more than a crate","A snapped rope sent the platform plunging down the shaft","Hoists were too slow to be worth the trouble"], 2, "Rope failure on a passenger hoist would have been fatal, so hoists carried only goods."],
    [1, "How did Otis's safety brake work?", ["A spring snapped out and caught ratchet teeth in the guide rails","A heavy counterweight pulled the platform back up if it fell","A second rope took over automatically when the first one broke","Air trapped in the shaft cushioned the platform's fall"], 0, "The brake stayed disengaged while the rope was taut and snapped into the rails the moment tension vanished."],
    [2, "Why did Otis have his rope cut in front of a crowd?", ["To test the strength of a new type of rope","To show how quickly a lift could be repaired","To win a prize for the fastest descent","To prove his brake worked and win public trust"], 3, "The public demonstration built the trust that turned an invention into orders."],
    [3, "How did safe lifts change the value of upper floors?", ["Lower floors became cheaper because of the street noise","Top floors went from the cheapest to the most prestigious","Rents rose evenly on every floor of a building","Upper floors stayed cheap because lifts were slow"], 1, "Once climbing stairs was unnecessary, the view and quiet made the top the best address."],
    [4, "What should you do if you get stuck in a lift?", ["Press the alarm or intercom, stay inside and wait for trained help","Force the doors open and climb out to the nearest floor","Press every button so that the lift restarts","Climb through the roof hatch to reach the shaft ladder"], 0, "The car is the safest place to be, and trained help will get you out."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"He cut his own rope in front of a crowd. Why he lived, and what it built."});

WB.L("tin-can", "explore", "beginner", "The tin can: food that keeps",
  "An army needed food that would not rot, so a French government offered a prize. The answer sits in your pantry today, and for decades nobody knew why it worked.",
  [
    ["A prize and some bottles", "France offered a prize for preserving food, and Nicolas Appert won it in 1810 by heating food sealed in glass bottles.",
      "For most of history, people preserved food by drying, salting, smoking, pickling or packing it in sugar. These methods changed taste and texture, and armies on the move still ate poorly. In the 1790s the French government offered a prize for a practical solution. (\"An army marches on its stomach\" is usually attributed to Napoleon, though nobody can prove he said it.)\n\nParisian confectioner Nicolas Appert spent about fifteen years experimenting. He packed food into thick glass bottles, sealed them with cork and wax, and heated them in boiling water for a carefully judged time. Samples sent to sea with the navy came back still edible months later. In 1810 he won 12,000 francs, on condition he published his method. His business is often called the first commercial cannery, though his containers were bottles, not cans."],
    ["Right method, wrong reason", "Appert's method worked because heat kills microbes and a seal keeps new ones out, which Pasteur's work explained decades later.",
      "Appert believed that heat drove out a corrupting quality in the air, and that sealing kept it from returning. He had the method right and the reason wrong. About half a century later, Louis Pasteur and others showed that food spoils mainly because of microorganisms: bacteria, yeasts and moulds. Heating kills most of them, and a proper seal stops new ones getting in. Without microbes, the food inside can last for years.\n\nThis is a lovely example of technology running ahead of science. People often learn that something works long before anyone can explain it. The explanation then lets engineers improve the method, for example by working out the precise temperatures and times different foods need."],
    ["From glass to tin", "Tin-plated iron cans began replacing fragile glass from 1810, but the can opener arrived almost fifty years later.",
      "Glass was heavy and broke easily on ships and battlefields. In 1810, the year Appert won, Englishman Peter Durand was granted a patent for preserving food in containers including tin-plated iron canisters. Bryan Donkin and partners set up a London canning works around 1812, supplying the Royal Navy and polar explorers. The cans were really iron, later steel, with a thin tin coating to stop rust, each soldered shut by hand.\n\nOddly, the can opener lagged behind. Early cans were so thick that instructions reportedly suggested a hammer and chisel, and soldiers reportedly used bayonets or even gunfire. Ezra Warner patented the first US opener in 1858, and William Lyman's rotating wheel followed in 1870, once thinner steel cans made openers practical."],
    ["Cans, war and lead", "Canned food fed explorers and armies, though lead solder in early cans may have harmed some and was later phased out.",
      "Canned food went wherever fresh supplies were scarce. Polar expeditions packed it in huge quantities. Sir John Franklin's doomed 1845 Arctic expedition carried thousands of cans, and some researchers suggest lead from the soldered seams contributed to the crew's decline, though historians debate how much it mattered compared with cold, scurvy and starvation.\n\nDuring the American Civil War, canned food reached soldiers on a large scale, and many took the habit home. By the late nineteenth century, factories using machines and pressure cookers called retorts turned out cans by the million. Lead solder was eventually phased out, and modern cans are often lined to protect both the food and the metal. Ring-pull lids came in the twentieth century."],
    ["The can in your cupboard", "Canned food is safe and nutritious, but discard cans that are swollen, leaking, badly dented or that spurt when opened.",
      "Today canned food is among the safest and cheapest ways to store nutritious food for long periods, and it keeps many nutrients surprisingly well. Canned tomatoes, beans and fish are kitchen staples around the world. Most cans are steel or aluminium, and both are highly recyclable.\n\nThe one serious danger is a rare bacterium, Clostridium botulinum, which can grow without oxygen if a can is poorly processed or damaged. So the advice is simple: do not use cans that are swollen, bulging, leaking, badly dented along the seams, or that spurt when opened. In a suspected case of food poisoning with serious symptoms, such as trouble breathing or swallowing, call 000."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What were the key steps in Appert's method?", ["Pack food in salt and store the jars in cool cellars","Dry food in the sun, then seal it in wax","Seal food in glass bottles, then heat them in boiling water","Boil food, then pour it into open jars"], 2, "Heating the sealed bottles for a judged time was the heart of Appert's preserving method."],
    [1, "Why does sealed, heated food keep without spoiling?", ["Heat kills most microbes and the seal stops new ones getting in","Heat drives out a corrupting quality in the air, as Appert believed","The tin coating gives off a natural preservative","Heat removes all moisture, and the seal keeps it out"], 0, "Appert had the method right but the reason wrong; Pasteur's work showed microbes cause spoilage."],
    [2, "Why did the can opener arrive so long after the can?", ["Soldiers preferred bayonets and refused to use new tools","Early cans were thick; openers needed thinner steel","Patent offices rejected opener designs for decades","Early cans were opened with a simple ring pull"], 1, "Opener design followed can design: thinner steel made a simple opener workable."],
    [3, "What risk did early cans carry?", ["The tin coating dissolved into the food as a poison","Rust from the iron caused rapid food spoilage","Pressure cookers left the food undercooked","Lead from soldered seams could get into the food"], 3, "Lead solder was eventually phased out and modern cans are often lined."],
    [4, "What should you do with a swollen or leaking can?", ["Cook it for longer to kill any bacteria inside","Use it quickly before the food spoils further","Throw it out, as it may harbour dangerous bacteria","Keep it, since swelling only affects the lid"], 2, "Damaged cans can harbour Clostridium botulinum, so safe practice is to discard them."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Food was sealed in cans for decades before anyone knew why it stayed fresh."});

WB.L("spectacles", "explore", "beginner", "Spectacles: the invention that extended working lives",
  "Nobody knows who invented glasses, and one famous inventor turns out to be a fake. But this medieval gadget quietly changed what people could do after forty.",
  [
    ["Ageing eyes and stones", "From the mid forties the eye's lens stiffens and close focus fails, which reading stones only partly solved before spectacles.",
      "Inside your eye, a flexible lens changes shape to focus at different distances. With age it stiffens, and by the mid forties most people find small print blurs unless held at arm's length. This is presbyopia, from the Greek for \"old eye\". Before spectacles, a scribe, jeweller or weaver could see their working life cut short at the peak of their skill.\n\nThe magnifying power of curved glass was known early. The Arab scholar Ibn al-Haytham studied optics around the year 1000, and his work was later translated into Latin. Medieval readers used \"reading stones\", rounded glass or rock crystal laid on the page. They helped, but you had to slide them along the line, and they were no use for sewing or carving."],
    ["Italy, and a fake inventor", "Spectacles appeared in northern Italy in the 1280s, their inventor is unknown, and the Salvino degli Armati story rests on a fabricated inscription.",
      "The best early evidence is a sermon. In 1306 the Dominican friar Giordano da Pisa told a Florentine congregation that eyeglasses had been invented less than twenty years earlier, and that he had met the maker, but he did not record the name. That points to northern Italy in the 1280s, where Venice had skilled glassworkers. The first spectacles had two convex lenses, thicker in the middle, in riveted frames perched on the nose.\n\nMany books once named Salvino degli Armati of Florence as the inventor. The evidence was a tomb inscription, which historians concluded was a seventeenth-century fabrication meant to give Florence the credit. No reliable earlier record exists. A satisfying story with a name and a plaque can travel for centuries, even when the honest answer is that we do not know."],
    ["Short sight and Kepler", "Concave lenses for short sight came later, and in 1604 Kepler explained that spectacles shift where light focuses on the retina.",
      "The first spectacles helped with close work, which is why they spread among older readers. Short-sighted people, who struggle with distant objects, needed concave lenses, thinner in the middle, which spread light out before it enters the eye. Clear evidence of these appears by the fifteenth century.\n\nSpectacle makers worked largely by trial and error. A proper theory came in 1604, when the astronomer Johannes Kepler showed that the eye forms an image on the retina at the back, and that spectacles work by shifting where light comes to a focus so it lands on the retina properly. Lens makers' skills also fed directly into the invention of the telescope and microscope."],
    ["Arms, bifocals, contacts", "Side arms, bifocals and contact lenses each solved a practical problem with wearing glasses.",
      "For centuries, the hardest part of wearing glasses was keeping them on. People tried ribbons, leather straps and frames that pinched the nose. Rigid side arms resting on the ears became common in the eighteenth century, and the London optician Edward Scarlett is often credited with popularising them in the first half of the 1700s.\n\nBenjamin Franklin, tired of swapping between two pairs, described his \"double spectacles\" in a 1784 letter, with each lens split into a top half for distance and a bottom half for reading. These became known as bifocals. In 1888 the German doctor Adolf Fick described glass lenses placed directly on the eye. Comfortable soft contact lenses arrived in the 1960s, thanks to the Czech chemist Otto Wichterle."],
    ["A quiet revolution", "Glasses extended skilled working lives and remain one of the most effective health tools, yet about a billion people have vision impairment that is preventable or unaddressed.",
      "It is easy to overlook glasses because they are everywhere, but their effect has been enormous. In medieval and early modern Europe, they let scholars, clerks, merchants and craftspeople keep doing detailed work for decades longer. Some historians argue this gave a small but real boost to learning and productivity, especially after the printing press made books cheaper and reading more common.\n\nThe need is still huge. The World Health Organization estimates that around a billion people worldwide have vision impairment that could have been prevented or has yet to be addressed, much of it simple short sight or presbyopia. A cheap pair of reading glasses can still keep someone working, reading and independent, just as it did seven centuries ago."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did spectacles matter so much to skilled workers?", ["They protected the eyes from dust and splinters of wood","Ageing eyes lose close focus; glasses restored working years","They allowed craftspeople to work comfortably in dim light","They improved the distance vision of scribes and merchants"], 1, "Presbyopia could end a craft career early, and spectacles removed that limit."],
    [1, "What should we conclude about the supposed inventor Salvino degli Armati?", ["He is well documented, but his workshop records were lost","He invented concave lenses rather than spectacles","He copied Ibn al-Haytham's design in Florence","His only evidence is a fabricated tomb inscription"], 3, "A tidy story with a name and a plaque can be false; the honest answer is that we do not know."],
    [2, "What do concave lenses correct, and how?", ["Short sight, by spreading light out before it enters the eye","Presbyopia, by thickening the eye's flexible lens","Long sight, by bringing light to a single point","Blurred reading, by simply magnifying the letters"], 0, "Concave lenses are thinner in the middle and suit people who struggle with distant objects."],
    [3, "Which problem did bifocals solve?", ["Glasses sliding off the nose","Lenses fogging up in cold weather","Swapping pairs for distance and reading","Frames pinching the bridge of the nose"], 2, "Each bifocal lens has a distance half on top and a reading half below."],
    [4, "What does the World Health Organization's estimate of a billion people show?", ["Most people now wear glasses by the age of thirty","Many vision problems, often simple ones, go unaddressed","Glasses cause more vision problems than they solve","Almost all vision impairment comes from untreatable disease"], 1, "Glasses are a cheap, effective health tool, and many people who need them still go without."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Nobody knows who invented glasses, and the best-known name rests on a forgery."});

WB.L("matthew-flinders", "explore", "beginner", "Matthew Flinders: the man who named Australia",
  "He sailed all the way around the continent, was locked up for six and a half years, and published his great work the day before he died. Then his grave was lost under a railway station.",
  [
    ["Tasmania is an island", "Flinders joined the navy at fifteen and, with George Bass, proved in 1798 and 1799 that Tasmania is an island.",
      "Matthew Flinders, born in 1774 in Lincolnshire, said Robinson Crusoe made him want to go to sea. He joined the Royal Navy at fifteen and sharpened his navigation and chart making under William Bligh on Bligh's second breadfruit voyage. In 1795 he reached the young colony of New South Wales and befriended the ship's surgeon George Bass, who shared his appetite for exploring the coast in tiny boats.\n\nEuropeans had long wondered whether Van Diemen's Land (Tasmania) joined the mainland. In 1798 and 1799 Flinders and Bass sailed the sloop Norfolk all the way around it, settling the question. The passage became Bass Strait, at Flinders' suggestion, giving ships from Britain a shorter if stormy route to Sydney."],
    ["Around the continent", "In 1801 to 1803 Flinders made the first known circumnavigation of Australia, with Bungaree and a ship's cat called Trim aboard.",
      "Flinders persuaded Sir Joseph Banks and the Admiralty to back a survey of the continent's coastline. He sailed in 1801 in HMS Investigator with scientists and artists, including botanist Robert Brown and botanical artist Ferdinand Bauer. He charted the south coast, then the east, rounding Cape York into the Gulf of Carpentaria. The ship was rotting so badly that he cut the survey short. He reached Sydney in June 1803, completing the first known voyage all the way around Australia.\n\nBungaree, a Kuring-gai man from the Broken Bay area, sailed with him and helped communicate with Aboriginal groups. He is regarded as the first Australian-born person to circumnavigate the continent. So did Trim, Flinders' clever black cat with white paws, who has statues near Flinders' own, including outside the State Library of NSW."],
    ["Shipwreck and prison", "After being wrecked on a reef in 1803, Flinders was detained by the French on Mauritius for six and a half years.",
      "In 1803 Flinders set off for England as a passenger aboard HMS Porpoise, which struck a reef off the Queensland coast, now called Wreck Reef. The survivors camped on a sandbank while Flinders took a ship's cutter, a small open boat, back to Sydney to organise a rescue. He then continued towards England in the tiny, badly leaking schooner Cumberland, and in December 1803 put in at Mauritius, then a French colony.\n\nBritain and France were at war again, and the governor, Charles Decaen, suspected him of spying. Flinders was detained for six and a half years, and used the time to work on his charts and journals. Trim, who had stayed with him, vanished on the island."],
    ["Naming Australia", "Flinders championed the name Australia, and Governor Macquarie began using it officially in 1817.",
      "Released in 1810, Flinders reached England in poor health and spent his last years preparing his account of the voyages. The continent was known by names including New Holland and Terra Australis, the \"southern land\". Flinders preferred a shorter form, Australia, which he found more pleasing to the ear. He was not the first to use the word, but he was its most influential champion.\n\nHis book, A Voyage to Terra Australis, used Australia in its text, though his publishers kept the older name in the title. Governor Lachlan Macquarie read it and began using Australia in official correspondence from 1817. The book was published on 18 July 1814. Flinders died the next day, aged forty."],
    ["Lost and found", "Flinders' grave was lost for more than two centuries until archaeologists found it near London's Euston station in 2019.",
      "Flinders was buried in a London cemetery, but over the following decades the burial ground was cleared and built over during the expansion of Euston railway station. His exact resting place was lost, and a story grew that he lay beneath one of the platforms.\n\nIn 2019, archaeologists excavating for the High Speed 2 rail project found a coffin with a lead breastplate bearing his name, among tens of thousands of burials. In 2024 his remains were reburied in his home village of Donington, Lincolnshire. In Australia his name lives on in places such as Flinders Street in Melbourne, the Flinders Ranges and Flinders University."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Flinders and Bass prove in 1798 and 1799?", ["A river route led from Sydney to an inland sea","Tasmania is an island, separated from the mainland by a strait","Van Diemen's Land was joined to Victoria by a narrow isthmus","New Holland and New South Wales were separate landmasses"], 1, "Sailing around Van Diemen's Land settled the question and opened a shorter route through Bass Strait."],
    [1, "What did the Investigator voyage achieve?", ["The first known voyage all the way around the Australian continent","The discovery of a new sea route between Sydney and Britain","The first survey of Tasmania's coastline","The founding of the first settlement in the north"], 0, "Flinders completed the circuit when he reached Sydney in June 1803."],
    [2, "Why was Flinders held on Mauritius for six and a half years?", ["He had been convicted of damaging a French ship","He was held until he handed over his charts","Britain and France were at war, and the governor suspected spying","His leaking schooner was seized for unpaid port fees"], 2, "Wartime suspicion, not any crime, kept him detained."],
    [3, "What was Flinders' role in how Australia got its name?", ["He invented the word, and the Admiralty adopted it in 1814","He argued against it and kept Terra Australis","Macquarie coined it after reading Bass's journals","He championed the shorter name, which Macquarie used from 1817"], 3, "Flinders was not the first to use the word, but he was its most influential champion."],
    [4, "How was Flinders' lost grave found?", ["Archaeologists on a rail project found a coffin with his name","Descendants found his burial record in a Lincolnshire church","A ground survey of a Donington churchyard located it","Builders at a Melbourne station dug up his remains"], 0, "Station expansion buried the cemetery, and the 2019 excavation uncovered his identifiable coffin."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Matthew Flinders mapped a continent. His grave was lost under a railway station."});

WB.L("zheng-he", "explore", "intermediate", "Zheng He and the treasure fleets",
  "Decades before Columbus, a Chinese admiral led some of the largest fleets the world had ever seen across the Indian Ocean. Then China stopped sending them.",
  [
    ["From captive to admiral", "Zheng He, a Muslim captive from Yunnan, rose through the household of Prince Zhu Di to command the Ming treasure fleets.",
      "Zheng He was born around 1371 in Yunnan, southwest China, into a Muslim family named Ma. His father and grandfather had reportedly made the pilgrimage to Mecca. When Ming armies conquered Yunnan, the boy was captured, castrated, as was common for captive boys entering royal service, and sent to serve the prince Zhu Di.\n\nHe grew into a tall, capable soldier and adviser and helped Zhu Di win a bloody civil war and seize the throne in 1402. Zhu Di became the Yongle Emperor, one of the most ambitious rulers in Chinese history. He rewarded his loyal eunuch with the surname Zheng, and later gave him command of an enterprise on a scale the world had rarely seen."],
    ["Seven great voyages", "Between 1405 and 1433 Zheng He led seven voyages with fleets of hundreds of ships across the Indian Ocean, though the size of the biggest ships is debated.",
      "The first voyage set out in 1405. Chinese records describe over 300 ships carrying around 27,000 people: sailors, soldiers, interpreters, doctors, craftsmen and officials. Across seven voyages, ending in 1433, the fleets visited Vietnam, Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand and Sri Lanka, then India's Calicut and Hormuz at the entrance to the Persian Gulf. Later voyages reached Arabia and East Africa, including Mogadishu and Malindi, and some crew visited Mecca. They sailed with the seasonal monsoon winds.\n\nThe largest \"treasure ships\" are described in a later Ming source as roughly 120 to 140 metres long. Some historians accept this; many doubt wooden hulls could be so long, and suggest 60 to 75 metres, still enormous. Nobody disputes the fleets were vast and well organised."],
    ["Prestige, not conquest", "The voyages displayed Ming power and drew rulers into China's tribute system, backed by force when needed, and one result was a giraffe at the Chinese court.",
      "The fleets did not set out to found colonies. Their main purpose was to show the new emperor's might and wealth, gather tribute, and bring foreign rulers into a system in which they acknowledged his supremacy and received gifts and trade in return. But the voyages were not entirely peaceful. Soldiers fought a pirate leader near Palembang in Sumatra, and in Sri Lanka a local king was captured and taken to China.\n\nOne charming result was a giraffe. In 1414 envoys from Bengal brought one to China, originally from Malindi. Courtiers declared it a qilin, a gentle creature of legend whose appearance signalled a wise and virtuous ruler. For an emperor who had taken power violently, heaven's approval walking into the palace on four long legs had obvious political value."],
    ["Why the voyages stopped", "After about 1433 China stopped sending fleets on that scale, for reasons of cost, frontier threats and hostile officials that historians still debate.",
      "Zheng He probably died around 1433, during or soon after the seventh voyage, and he may have been buried at sea. No fleet on that scale ever sailed again. Historians point to several reasons. The voyages were extremely expensive, and the court faced costly pressures on land, especially from Mongol forces on the northern frontier and the building of the new capital at Beijing.\n\nConfucian officials were often hostile to the powerful palace eunuchs who ran the voyages, and saw them as a wasteful vanity. Later Ming governments also restricted private overseas trade for long periods. A story says officials deliberately destroyed the voyage records, though how much was lost and why is still debated."],
    ["Myth and memory", "Zheng He did not reach America, as a 2002 book claimed; he is honoured across Asia as a symbol of seafaring and cultural exchange.",
      "Zheng He's story has attracted wild claims. A popular book published in 2002 argued that his fleets discovered America and sailed around the world in 1421. Historians have firmly rejected this: there is no credible evidence for it in Chinese or other records, or in archaeology. The real story needs no exaggeration.\n\nAround Southeast Asia, temples and shrines remember him, including in Malacca, Semarang and Surabaya, and he is respected by both Chinese and Muslim communities. In modern China he is a symbol of peaceful maritime exchange. Decades before Portuguese ships reached India, Chinese fleets had crossed the same ocean, and then chose not to come back."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Zheng He come to command the fleets?", ["He was a Ming admiral trained in the imperial navy","He was a captive who helped Prince Zhu Di win the throne","He was a merchant who funded the fleet himself","He inherited the command from his father"], 1, "Zhu Di became the Yongle Emperor and rewarded his loyal eunuch with command."],
    [1, "What is debated about the treasure ships?", ["Whether any ships left China at all","Whether the fleets used the monsoon winds","Whether the biggest reached 120 metres or more","Whether the fleets ever visited Africa"], 2, "Wooden hulls that long would flex and leak, so many historians think the figures are exaggerated."],
    [2, "What was the main purpose of the voyages?", ["To display Ming power and gather tribute","To found colonies along the Indian Ocean coast","To search for a sea route to Europe","To spread the emperor's religion abroad"], 0, "The fleets were floating diplomacy, backed by force when needed."],
    [3, "Why did China's great ocean voyages end?", ["A storm destroyed the whole fleet in 1433","Portuguese ships blocked the Indian Ocean","The monsoon winds stopped reversing","Cost, hostile officials and pressures on land"], 3, "Historians point to expense, frontier threats and opposition at court, though the details are debated."],
    [4, "What do historians say about the claim that Zheng He's fleets reached America in 1421?", ["Chinese records confirm it, but only for some trips","Historians reject it: no credible evidence exists","Wrecks found on the Pacific coast prove it","It rests on a hidden set of official Ming maps"], 1, "The real story of the fleets needs no exaggeration."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A fleet of hundreds of Chinese ships crossed the Indian Ocean in 1405. Then it stopped."});

WB.L("standard-time", "explore", "intermediate", "How railways invented time zones",
  "Once every town set its clocks by its own sun. Then trains arrived, timetables turned into chaos, and the world had to agree on what time it was.",
  [
    ["Trains break local time", "Each town once kept its own solar noon, and fast trains with precise timetables made that confusing and dangerous.",
      "For most of history, time was local. Noon was when the Sun stood highest, and because the Earth turns, that moment arrives later the further west you go. Bristol's clocks ran about ten minutes behind London's. By horse and coach, nobody cared.\n\nRailways changed the scale. People covered in hours what once took days, and timetables had to be precise to the minute. With every station on local time, passengers missed connections, and on single track lines confusion over time could cause collisions. The telegraph made a solution possible, because a time signal could travel along wires almost instantly. Railway companies became the keepers of the clock and ran their whole networks on one standard time."],
    ["Railway time", "Railways, not governments, pushed Britain and North America onto standard time: Greenwich time in the 1840s and the day of two noons in 1883.",
      "In Britain, the Great Western Railway adopted London time in 1840, and by about 1848 most railways used Greenwich Mean Time. Some towns kept two times for a while, with public clocks given two minute hands. In 1880 Parliament made GMT the legal time across Great Britain.\n\nNorth America had a bigger problem: dozens of railway times, with big stations showing several clocks. On 18 November 1883 the railways of the United States and Canada switched to standard time zones about an hour apart. In many towns clocks were reset at noon, so people lived through noon twice, which is why it is remembered as the day of two noons. The railways, not the government, organised it. US federal law made time zones official only in 1918."],
    ["Choosing Greenwich", "In 1884 an international conference chose Greenwich as zero longitude, giving countries a shared reference on which to build time zones.",
      "Standard zones need a starting point. In 1884 delegates from about twenty five countries met in Washington DC for the International Meridian Conference. They chose the meridian through the Royal Observatory at Greenwich as zero degrees longitude. Practical reasons favoured it, as most of the world's shipping already used charts based on Greenwich.\n\nThe Canadian railway engineer Sandford Fleming was one of the great champions of a worldwide system of time based on a single meridian. Not everyone agreed: France abstained in the vote and kept using the Paris meridian for its own purposes for decades. The conference did not impose time zones around the world, but it gave countries a shared reference to build their own on."],
    ["Australia's time zones", "The Australian colonies adopted standard time in 1895, and today daylight saving differences leave the mainland on five different times in summer.",
      "In Australia, each colony had kept its own local time, and travellers crossing borders by train faced the same headaches as everyone else. In 1895 the colonies agreed on standard times based on Greenwich: Eastern, Central and Western. South Australia first chose nine hours ahead of Greenwich, but in 1899 moved to nine and a half hours, a choice that has been debated ever since. The Northern Territory, then administered by South Australia, followed.\n\nHalf-hour zones are unusual but not unique: India uses one, and Nepal goes further with a quarter-hour offset. Daylight saving complicates things today. Queensland, Western Australia and the Northern Territory don't use it, so in summer the mainland runs on five different times."],
    ["Time today", "Modern time is set by atomic clocks, but time zones remain a mix of geography and politics.",
      "Greenwich Mean Time has largely been replaced as the world's reference by Coordinated Universal Time, or UTC, kept by a network of highly accurate atomic clocks. Because the Earth's spin is slightly irregular, leap seconds have occasionally been added to keep UTC close to the Sun, but in 2022 international scientists voted to stop adding them by 2035.\n\nTime zones themselves are decided by governments, and politics often beats geography. China, which spans the width of five standard zones, uses just one official time, so in its far west the Sun can rise very late by the clock. Spain sits mostly west of Greenwich but uses Central European Time. The railway engineers' solution still governs every phone and laptop."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did local solar time become a problem?", ["Towns began setting their clocks by different calendars","Fast trains and timetables made differing town times unsafe","Clocks stopped being accurate once railways opened","Church bells could no longer be heard from stations"], 1, "Local time was harmless at horse speed, but trains needed one shared clock."],
    [1, "Why is 18 November 1883 remembered as the day of two noons?", ["Clocks were reset at noon, so noon came twice","Railways ran a second set of trains at noon that day","The Sun appeared to stand still over the continent","Governments declared a second midday holiday"], 0, "Railways switched North America to standard zones, and many towns reset their clocks at noon."],
    [2, "What did the 1884 International Meridian Conference achieve?", ["It imposed one set of time zones on every country","It abolished local solar time in all member nations","It moved the prime meridian from Paris to Washington","It chose Greenwich as zero longitude for time zones"], 3, "The conference gave countries a common starting line, but left them to build their own zones."],
    [3, "Why does the Australian mainland run on five times in summer?", ["Each state sets its own calendar year","Some places use daylight saving and others do not","The 1884 conference fixed five Australian zones","Sunrise varies too much to use fewer zones in summer"], 1, "Zone differences plus daylight saving choices produce five different clock times."],
    [4, "Why does China use a single time zone across its whole width?", ["Atomic clocks drift unevenly across big countries","UTC requires large countries to use fewer zones","Governments decide zones, and politics often beats geography","The Earth spins more slowly in the east"], 2, "Time zones are political choices, as China's one zone and Spain's Central European Time show."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In 1883, many towns lived through noon twice. Here's why."});

WB.L("lewis-and-clark", "explore", "intermediate", "Lewis and Clark: across a continent",
  "In 1804 a small army party set out to cross North America to the Pacific and back. They found no easy river route, but they mapped a continent and depended on the people who already lived there.",
  [
    ["Jefferson's big question", "After the Louisiana Purchase, President Jefferson sent Lewis and Clark to explore the west and find a water route to the Pacific.",
      "In 1803 the United States bought the Louisiana Territory from France for about fifteen million dollars, roughly doubling the young country. President Jefferson wanted to know what the land held, from plants and rivers to the Native nations living there. Above all he hoped for a practical water route linking the Missouri to the Pacific. He chose his secretary, army captain Meriwether Lewis, to lead.\n\nLewis asked his former army comrade William Clark to share command, and the two worked as genuine equals. Lewis spent months studying botany, astronomy and medicine and buying supplies, including gifts for Native leaders. The Corps of Discovery, thirty to forty soldiers and boatmen, with York, an enslaved man owned by Clark, and Lewis's dog Seaman, left near St Louis in May 1804."],
    ["Winter, and Sacagawea", "The Corps wintered with the Mandan and Hidatsa, where Sacagawea joined; she was an interpreter and a sign of peace, not the guide.",
      "By late 1804 the Corps had travelled over two thousand kilometres up the Missouri to the Mandan and Hidatsa villages in what is now North Dakota, and built a fort to survive the winter. Only one member died on the whole journey, Sergeant Charles Floyd. There they hired the trader Toussaint Charbonneau, whose wife Sacagawea was a young Shoshone woman captured by Hidatsa raiders as a girl. Her baby son travelled with them.\n\nSacagawea is often pictured as the guide, but she was not hired as one and did not know most of the route. Her value lay elsewhere: she spoke Shoshone, recognised landmarks near her home, and her presence signalled peaceful intent, since war parties did not travel with women. In August 1805 the Shoshone chief Cameahwait proved to be her brother, which helped secure horses."],
    ["No easy passage", "The Rocky Mountains were far wider and harder than Jefferson expected, ending the dream of an easy water route across the continent.",
      "The first great obstacle was the Great Falls of the Missouri, where it took about a month to carry boats and supplies around a series of waterfalls. Then came the Rockies. Jefferson had hoped for a short portage between rivers, but the mountains were range after range. In September 1805 the Corps crossed the Bitterroot Mountains on a steep trail in early snow, with little game. They were reduced to eating candles and some of their horses.\n\nStarving and sick, they came down into the lands of the Nez Perce, who fed them and helped them build canoes. They paddled down the Snake and Columbia rivers and in November 1805 reached the Pacific. The crossing ended the dream of an easy water route."],
    ["A vote on the coast", "At the Pacific the captains let the whole party vote on where to winter, including York and Sacagawea, though it changed nothing about their status.",
      "The Corps had to decide where to spend the winter, and the captains put the question to the whole party. York's view and Sacagawea's were recorded alongside the soldiers', an extraordinary moment for an enslaved man and a Native woman in that era, though it changed nothing about their status afterwards.\n\nThey built Fort Clatsop, near today's Astoria in Oregon, and endured a wet, miserable winter, with rain on most days. In March 1806 they started for home. The return journey included a deadly clash with Blackfeet warriors, the only fight of the journey in which people were killed."],
    ["What the journey left", "The Corps' maps and descriptions of over a hundred new species transformed knowledge of the west, but the expedition opened a painful chapter for Native nations.",
      "The Corps reached St Louis in September 1806, after more than two years away, to a public that had largely given them up for dead. Their journals and Clark's maps transformed knowledge of the west, and they described well over a hundred animals and plants new to science, including the grizzly bear and prairie dog.\n\nBut the expedition also opened the way for fur traders, settlers and the US government, and over the following century Native nations along the route lost much of their land and many lives. The travellers' fates were mixed. Lewis died of gunshot wounds in 1809, widely believed to be suicide. Clark did not free York for around a decade. Sacagawea is thought to have died young, around 1812, though some Shoshone traditions say she lived into old age."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Jefferson most hope the expedition would find?", ["A chain of sites for forts to defend the territory","A water route linking the Missouri River to the Pacific","Gold deposits to fund the new government","A treaty line separating the territory from Spain"], 1, "The hoped-for water route was the expedition's central geographic goal."],
    [1, "What was Sacagawea's real value to the expedition?", ["She spoke Shoshone and her presence signalled peaceful intent","She knew the full route and guided the Corps across the Rockies","She was an army scout hired to hunt food","She was a trained mapmaker who drew the charts"], 0, "The popular picture of Sacagawea as guide is a myth; her skills and presence mattered in other ways."],
    [2, "What did the Rocky Mountains show about the hoped-for route?", ["A short portage linked the rivers, as Jefferson expected","The Missouri flowed straight through to the Pacific","A pass known to Native guides gave an easy crossing","No easy route existed; the mountains were far wider than hoped"], 3, "Range after range of mountains ended the dream of a simple river passage."],
    [3, "What made the vote on where to winter remarkable?", ["It was the only time the captains gave up command","Native leaders chose the site of Fort Clatsop","An enslaved man and a Native woman had their views recorded","The whole party voted to head home early"], 2, "Recording their views was extraordinary for the era, even though it changed nothing for them afterwards."],
    [4, "What was the expedition's lasting effect on Native nations?", ["It secured permanent protection for Native lands","It opened the way for settlers, and nations lost land and lives","It had little effect, since the Corps left no maps","It ended contact for decades as the route was too hard"], 1, "The maps and knowledge brought by the Corps served expansion that cost Native nations dearly."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"She was not the guide. The real story of Sacagawea is more interesting."});

WB.L("challenger-deep", "explore", "intermediate", "Into the Challenger Deep",
  "The deepest point in the ocean lies nearly 11 kilometres down, under pressure that could crush a submarine. Two men first reached it in 1960 in a craft held up by petrol.",
  [
    ["The deepest place", "The Challenger Deep is nearly 11 kilometres down, where pressure is over a thousand times that at the surface.",
      "In the western Pacific lies the Mariana Trench, a scar on the seafloor over 2,500 kilometres long, formed where one tectonic plate dives beneath another. At its southern end is the Challenger Deep, the deepest known place in the oceans, about 10,900 metres down by most recent measurements. Put Mount Everest at the bottom and its summit would still sit more than two kilometres underwater. It is named after HMS Challenger, whose 1870s scientific voyage took a sounding there.\n\nEvery ten metres of seawater adds roughly one atmosphere of pressure, so at the bottom it is more than a thousand times that at sea level, over a tonne on every square centimetre. An ordinary submarine would be crushed."],
    ["The bathyscaphe", "Piccard's bathyscaphe paired a thick crew sphere with a petrol-filled float, because petrol is lighter than water and barely compresses.",
      "A crewed vessel needs a thick sphere, which spreads pressure evenly, but such a sphere sinks like a stone. The question was how to float back up. The solution came from the Swiss physicist Auguste Piccard, who had flown balloons into the stratosphere. His bathyscaphe, meaning \"deep boat\", was essentially an underwater balloon: a large petrol-filled float above a small, thick steel crew sphere.\n\nPetrol is lighter than water and compresses very little, so it keeps providing lift under enormous pressure. To descend, the craft carried tonnes of iron pellets as ballast, held by electromagnets. To rise, the crew released the pellets. Auguste and his son Jacques built the Trieste in Italy in the 1950s, and the US Navy later bought it."],
    ["23 January 1960", "In 1960 Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep in the Trieste, staying about twenty minutes.",
      "On 23 January 1960, Jacques Piccard and US Navy lieutenant Don Walsh squeezed into the Trieste's cramped sphere, only about two metres across inside. The descent took nearly five hours. Partway down they heard a loud crack, and later found that an outer window had fractured, though the inner structure held.\n\nThey settled onto the seabed, nearly 11 kilometres down, and spent only around twenty minutes there. Their landing stirred up fine sediment that made photographs impossible. Piccard reported seeing a flatfish through the porthole, but many biologists now doubt this, since fish are not thought to survive at that depth. The ascent took over three hours, and both men returned to a hero's welcome."],
    ["Fifty-two years later", "No one returned for 52 years, until James Cameron's 2012 solo dive in a submersible built mostly in Sydney using buoyant foam.",
      "After the Trieste, no human went back for fifty-two years. Uncrewed vehicles such as Japan's Kaiko reached the deep in the 1990s, while crewed submersibles like Alvin explored shallower sites. Attention flowed to the Space Race, and it was said that more people had walked on the Moon than visited the deepest ocean.\n\nOn 26 March 2012 filmmaker James Cameron dived alone in Deepsea Challenger, a vertical, torpedo-like submersible designed and built mostly in Sydney, with Australian engineer Ron Allum. It used syntactic foam, packed with tiny hollow glass spheres, for buoyancy instead of a petrol float. The descent took about two and a half hours, and he spent around three hours at the bottom before a hydraulic fault cut his time short. He described a lunar-like, desolate landscape."],
    ["The deep becomes reachable", "Since 2019 a reusable submersible has taken many people to the bottom, and has found plastic debris even there.",
      "In 2019 the American explorer Victor Vescovo began repeated dives in the Limiting Factor, a titanium-hulled submersible designed to reach the bottom of any ocean again and again. On his Five Deeps Expedition he reached the deepest points of all five oceans. Over the following years the craft took a number of people to the Challenger Deep. In June 2020, Kathryn Sullivan, a former NASA astronaut and the first American woman to walk in space, became the first woman to reach it.\n\nDives have also brought sobering news, as researchers have reported plastic debris and human-made pollutants even in the deepest trenches. The bottom of the world is no longer out of reach, and neither, it turns out, is our rubbish."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can't an ordinary submarine reach the Challenger Deep?", ["Its engines would overheat in the cold water","The trench is too narrow for a submarine to enter","Pressure over a thousand times sea level would crush it","It would lose all navigation without sunlight"], 2, "Pressure rises by about one atmosphere every ten metres, so the bottom is crushing."],
    [1, "Why is petrol used in a bathyscaphe's float?", ["It is lighter than water and barely compresses","It burns to push the craft upwards","It is denser than seawater and keeps the craft stable","It insulates the crew sphere from the cold"], 0, "Lift that survives enormous pressure is what lets the craft return to the surface."],
    [2, "What happened during the Trieste's 1960 descent?", ["The petrol float leaked and had to be replaced","An outer window cracked, though the inner structure held","The iron ballast jammed and slowed the dive","The crew sphere briefly flooded"], 1, "The dive succeeded despite the crack, and the crew still reached the bottom."],
    [3, "How did Deepsea Challenger differ from the Trieste?", ["It used buoyant foam instead of a petrol float","It was lowered on a long cable from a ship","It carried two crew in a spherical cabin","It descended using iron pellet ballast"], 0, "Syntactic foam, packed with hollow glass spheres, gave buoyancy at full ocean depth."],
    [4, "What have recent dives found in the deepest trenches?", ["No sign of any life at all","Large shipwrecks from the Second World War","Sunlight reaching the seabed from vents","Plastic debris and human-made pollutants"], 3, "Even the bottom of the ocean now shows human impact."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The deepest point of the ocean sat unvisited for 52 years after 1960."});

WB.L("scurvy-vitamin-c", "explore", "advanced", "Scurvy: the cure that kept getting lost",
  "For centuries a disease killed more sailors than storms and battles. The cure was found, forgotten and found again, and the final answer turned on a lucky choice of guinea pig.",
  [
    ["The sailor's plague", "Scurvy, caused by a lack of vitamin C, killed huge numbers of sailors by weakening the collagen that holds the body together.",
      "After a couple of months on salted meat and ship's biscuit, sailors grew exhausted. Their gums swelled and bled, teeth loosened, bruises appeared from nowhere and old wounds reopened. Even long-healed fractures could come apart. Untreated, scurvy killed. On George Anson's voyage around the world in the 1740s, about 1,900 men set out and only a few hundred returned, with disease, mostly scurvy, killing far more than enemy action. Vasco da Gama also lost many men to it.\n\nThe cause is a lack of vitamin C, which helps build collagen, the protein that holds skin, vessels, gums and scars together. Without it, tiny vessels leak and wounds cannot knit. Humans, other apes, guinea pigs and some bats and birds cannot make vitamin C, so we must eat it."],
    ["Lind's experiment", "In 1747 James Lind ran an early controlled trial showing citrus cured scurvy, but the lesson did not stick.",
      "In 1747 the naval surgeon James Lind chose twelve sailors with scurvy and split them into six pairs. On the same basic diet, each pair received a different remedy: cider, dilute sulphuric acid, vinegar, seawater, a spice and garlic paste, or two oranges and a lemon daily. The citrus pair improved dramatically, one being fit for duty within about a week. Comparing treatments side by side in similar patients makes this a landmark of medicine.\n\nYet Lind treated citrus as one remedy among many. He also boiled juice into a concentrate, and vitamin C breaks down with heat, so it was far weaker than fresh fruit. Nobody knew why citrus worked, and James Cook credited several remedies, including sauerkraut. His fresh food from ashore probably mattered more."],
    ["Lemons, then limes", "The Royal Navy's lemon juice largely ended scurvy from 1795, but a later switch to weaker limes let it return.",
      "Urged on by the physician Gilbert Blane and others, the Royal Navy began issuing lemon juice to all sailors in 1795, usually in the daily grog. Scurvy largely vanished from the fleet. Then the cure was quietly undermined. In the mid nineteenth century the navy switched to cheaper limes from the West Indies. Limes contain considerably less vitamin C than lemons, and processing the juice could destroy more.\n\nBritish sailors earned the nickname limeys, but on long Arctic expeditions in the 1870s scurvy struck men who had taken their lime juice. Many experts wrongly concluded that citrus did not work. Others blamed bacteria or poisons in tinned meat."],
    ["A lucky guinea pig", "In 1907 guinea pigs, which like us cannot make vitamin C, developed scurvy, and that opened the way to identifying the vitamin.",
      "The breakthrough came in 1907. The Norwegian scientists Axel Holst and Theodor Frølich were studying beriberi and fed guinea pigs a grain-only diet. Instead the animals developed scurvy. It was luck: rats and mice make their own vitamin C and would have shown nothing. Now scurvy could be studied in the lab and foods could be tested for their power to prevent it.\n\nIn the late 1920s Albert Szent-Györgyi isolated a compound that, in the early 1930s, he showed prevented scurvy. It was renamed ascorbic acid, meaning \"against scurvy\". Norman Haworth worked out its structure and helped synthesise it, and in 1937 Szent-Györgyi and Haworth won Nobel Prizes."],
    ["Scurvy today", "Scurvy is rare but not gone, and its history shows a cure is only as good as our understanding of why it works.",
      "With vitamin C cheap and plentiful in fruit and vegetables, scurvy is rare in wealthy countries, but it has not disappeared. Doctors still see it in people with severely limited diets, such as some older people living alone, people with alcohol dependence or certain eating disorders, and occasionally children with very selective eating. Because it is now unusual, it can be missed: bleeding gums and bruising have many causes. Treatment with vitamin C, guided by a doctor, usually brings rapid improvement.\n\nThe story carries a lasting lesson. Lind found strong evidence in 1747, yet confusion, cost-cutting and wrong theories meant sailors kept dying for generations. A cure is only as good as our understanding of why it works."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a lack of vitamin C cause bleeding gums and slow-healing wounds?", ["Collagen forms poorly, so tissues and vessels break down","The blood cannot carry enough iron to the gums","Bacteria multiply unchecked in the mouth","The skin loses its natural oils and dries out"], 0, "Vitamin C helps build collagen, and without it tissues, vessels and scars fall apart."],
    [1, "What made Lind's 1747 experiment a landmark?", ["It compared treatments side by side in similar patients","It tested one remedy on an entire ship's crew","It identified vitamin C as the active ingredient","It ended scurvy in the navy within a year"], 0, "Fair side-by-side comparison is the core of a controlled trial, even in a very small one."],
    [2, "Why did scurvy return after the navy switched from lemon to lime juice?", ["Sailors refused to drink the lime juice","Limes cause a different disease","Limes held less vitamin C than lemons","Limes were never issued on long voyages"], 2, "Limes contain considerably less vitamin C than lemons, so the cure quietly weakened."],
    [3, "Why was the choice of guinea pigs so lucky for researchers?", ["Guinea pigs are the only animals that eat grain","They develop beriberi within days","They reproduce faster than rats and mice","Like humans, they cannot make their own vitamin C"], 3, "Animals that make their own vitamin C, like rats and mice, would not have developed scurvy."],
    [4, "What lasting lesson does the history of scurvy offer?", ["Evidence is enough on its own for a cure to be adopted","A cure is only as good as our understanding of why it works","New treatments should never be tested on small groups","Diet matters less than medicine for deadly diseases"], 1, "Lind had strong evidence in 1747, but without understanding the reason, the cure was lost and sailors kept dying."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A cure for a deadly disease was found, lost and found again. Why?"});

